response-og: Here is a new candidate configuration that builds on the best‐performing setting we have so far (left action – velocity weight = –1, right action – position weight ≈ 0.5, velocity weight ≈ 1) and nudges the velocity weight for the “right” action a little higher:

**Proposed parameters (in order = [ p0, p1, p2, p3, p4, p5, p6, p7, p8 ]):**

```
[ 0.0, -1.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.5, 1.2, 0.0, -1.0, 0.0 ]
```

**Why this set?**

| Index | Meaning                               | Chosen value | Rationale |
|------|----------------------------------------|--------------|-----------|
| p0   | Position weight for “left” action      | 0.0          | No direct position influence needed for left. |
| p1   | Velocity weight for “left” action      | –1.0         | Mirrors the best‑so‑far policy (push left when velocity is negative). |
| p2‑p3| Weights for the “neutral” action       | 0.0, 0.0     | Keeps the neutral action constantly less attractive (bias –1). |
| p4   | Position weight for “right” action     | 0.5          | Tested values showed 0.5 yields the lowest episode length. |
| p5   | Velocity weight for “right” action     | 1.2          | Slight increase over the previous 1.0 – aims to make the right action fire a bit earlier when the car gains speed, potentially reducing steps. |
| p6   | Bias for “left” action                 | 0.0          | Keeps left action available whenever its score (‑vel) exceeds the others. |
| p7   | Bias for “neutral” action              | –1.0         | Keeps the neutral action unused (as in the best run). |
| p8   | Bias for “right” action                | 0.0          | No shift in the right‑action threshold – avoids the premature‑right failures seen with larger positive biases. |

This configuration has not been tried before (the only change from the current best –115.2 reward run is the velocity weight for the right action, increased from 1.0 to 1.2). According to the trends observed in the previous data, a modest increase in the velocity weight for the right action can help the agent switch to “right” a little sooner after it has built enough leftward momentum, potentially shaving a few steps off the episode length while still keeping the failure‑rate low.
